## Live learnings — do not re-derive, do not re-chase

**Metadata / Raft**
- The apply IS the allocator: the CREATE/MKDIR command's ino is a hint
  (`alloc_hint_or_next`), honored only if ≥ watermark and unoccupied;
  hashed-create / cross-group-mkdir txns CAS the ALLOC key; log-path allocs
  are BUSY under a pending intent and bump the key version
  (`alloc_key_claim`). Callers read the ino back from the dentry.
- Same-parent concurrent dir ops no longer conflict on the parent row
 (§7.2 reductions, below). A dir-op txn still lands STALE/BUSY on a real
 conflict — same name, same child row, emptiness witness moved — and the
 client retries (`stale_retryable`: CREATE/UNLINK/LINK/RENAME_AT).
- **Parent row / dseq / dir-lane are commutative reductions (§7.2, Sep 21):**
 `EFS_TXN_REDUCE_INO` (signed nlink/nents delta, times MAX, used_shards
 OR, parent SET) and `EFS_TXN_REDUCE_ADD` (dseq +1) fold into whatever the
 key holds at RESOLVE (`fold_reduce`, dispatch by DATA key kind) and bump
 its version. A pending reduce and an EXCL/GUARD on one key are mutually
 BUSY; the log path probes before an unversioned DEL (`dir_txn_busy`,
 `efs_txn_key_busy`) and returns BUSY; dseq GUARDs compare the observed
 VALUE (`efs_txn_dseq_observe`), never the version. Never reintroduce a
 full-image EXCL PUT of a parent row — it was the Sep 20 lost update
 (`test-dir.0-0` nlink=2 with 3 children, rmdir EIO forever). Client
 retries STALE for CREATE/UNLINK/LINK/RENAME_AT. Repro/gate:
 `tests/stress/same_parent_storm.sh` (parent must end `children=0
 nlink=2`, rmdir OK). The old flat ~140 ops/s was one AppendEntries
 per op (a nested proposer broadcast under `h->mu`). Sep 27:
 `results/measure/20260927-211953-samedir-rate` is 333/1385/1241.
 Do not put every proposer on the fsync hold (idle mkdir 7.1 → 11.9 ms).
- **Never scan a whole shard (2-byte prefix) in the apply path.**
  `efs_txn_resolve`/`efs_txn_drop` did (0.7–1.1 s per RESOLVE on the
  root's shard, every replica, under the KV lock and `h->mu`) → the
  bimodal 2 ms / 1 s root mkdir, 400 ms `host_wait_applied` BUSY, 10.4 s
  client backoff, retried committed UNLINK → ENOENT. Fixed `165e779`
  (`txn_scan_kinds`: three 3-byte prefixes). Bound every scan by kind.
  **Latent hazard, now the open I16 item:** a BUSY from the apply-wait
  deadline makes the client retry a non-idempotent op (LINK/UNLINK/
  MKDIR/RENAME) that may already have committed → EEXIST/ENOENT/EIO.
- **A txn record with no coordinator behind it is permanent poison until
  recovery removes it** (a coordinator BUSY on its own DECIDE/RESOLVE
  wait left INTENT/GUARD/REDUCE behind; an EXCL intent on a shard's
  ALLOC key made every log-path create/mkdir there BUSY; 105 orphans,
  `results/measure/20260921-w8-orphans`). Since `9534e53`/`3291c6d` the
  group leader's GC thread (`host_txn_recover_pass`) scans a shard's
  three txn-record prefixes once its PREPARE-apply mark is 5 s old and
  DECIDEs/RESOLVEs (PROTO = it was COMMITted); logs `txn-recover`.
  Diagnose with `tests/tools/kv_intents` on a COPY of `mdraft/kv`. A
  `(retry next pass)` line every 5 s = coordinator group without a leader.
  Since the Sep 26 finisher, `host_txn_commit` also queues a txn whose
  DECIDE/RESOLVE wait returned BUSY; the finisher thread reads the
  decision and proposes RESOLVE (retries BUSY for 10 s). `raft-obs`
  prints `fin_q` / `fin_done` / `fin_drop`. A stall longer than 400 ms
  is still the compaction (`apply_max`, `persist_max` ~1 ms) — W13.
- **Never run KV prefix scans in a loop on a server thread.** One
 `efs_kv_scan_prefix` = one block `pread` per LSM segment whose key
 range can hold the prefix (all of them before Sep 26; now L0s plus
 one or two L1s), under the LSM mutex the apply path needs; the first recovery
  sweep (1 536/s per leader, 3 % CPU) put every metadata op at ~100 ms
  (`results/measure/20260921-w8-orphans/sweep-regression.txt`). Lock
  hold, not CPU. Check `python3 tests/measure/md_latency.py` (reference
  medians 6.1/4.0/6.2/0.3/1.5/5.2 ms since `84a2a55`) after any change
  that adds background KV work.
- **A `kv_seg` iterator reads one block; the 1 MiB readahead is opt-in
 (`kv_seg_iter_set_seq`, compaction and export only; Sep 30,
 `44f397b4`).** Every prefix scan under `l->mu` (getattr's per-lane
 `efs_txn_reduce_read_ex`, GC/REAP/txn-recover, dir lookups) opened
 an iterator per covering segment and each `iter_load` pulled 1 MiB:
 one stat of a 64-lane file was 2 × 22 × 1 MiB of pread on the
 server (9.8 ms, servers at 2 % CPU). Same review:
 `host_read_inode_lanes` did a ReadIndex per active foreign lane (32
 peer round trips per stat on a follower) — one per group now. A
 big-file stat is 4.7 ms after both; the rest is the 64-lane double
 collect (design). GC frag pass was 32 records + one 16-ack entry
 per group per second = 16 fragments/s (21 h per 100 GiB file); now
 256/128 (`EFS_META_GC_ACK_MAX`), rescan while full within 200 ms.
 `raft-host: gc-pass ms= reap= frag= spread= recover=` prints when a
 GC iteration exceeds 5 ms; leaders show `recover=70`, 64 ms of it
 `HOST_REC_YIELD_US` sleeps.
- `efs_kv_scan_prefix` callback returning **>0 means batch-full**, not
 error. The reaper (`lane-sweep`, `rsv_purge`) misread it for weeks —
 no lane with >64 chunks was ever swept. Check this in any new scan user.
- **Lane shards straddle both groups** (`lane_shard = ish + lane × odd
 stride`): every odd lane of an inode is in the other group. Anything the
 anchor-group leader drives per lane (reaper `LANE_SWEEP`) must forward
 foreign-group commands (`host_gc_propose` → `host_remote_cmd`, Sep 20);
 leader-only made every inode with an active odd lane unreapable, and the
 retry stream (92 % of the log, 33 entries/s per group, idle cluster)
 queued every client commit behind it.
 **Before any latency or metadata measurement: `commit` flat for 30 s on
 both groups, and `tests/tools/raft_log_tail.py` on the leader's
 `raft.log` shows no command dominating.**
- **The ~100 ms metadata floor (mkdir med 103 ms, append+close 170) was a
 wakeup bug, not fsync:** raft_host's per-peer sender threads all waited on
 one shared condvar with `cond_signal`, so a queued AE/AE-reply usually
 woke the wrong sender and left at the next 50 ms heartbeat, 0–50 ms per
 hop. Per-peer `tx[].cv` since Sep 20: mkdir med **7.2 ms**, create+close
 6.7, append+close 9.0, stat 0.4 (`results/perf/20260921-md-latency.txt`).
 A raft commit costs ~2–6 ms here; a median far above that on an idle
 cluster is a scheduling/wakeup bug — strace the FOLLOWER (acks every
 other heartbeat = this class) before blaming fsync.
- **Handlers never take `h->mu` to read raft state or to wait** (`4eb1419`).
 The pump publishes commit/applied/term/role/leader/read-round as atomics
 (`host_publish_view`); readers use the view and sleep on `applied_cv`
 under `cv_mu`; `h->mu` only to propose or read_begin. Before, 30+
 handlers per cycle sat on the pump's mutex, it lost its heartbeat
 cadence and both groups re-elected 4× in 50 s under the 9-host suite.
 Waiters sleep in per-waiter slots (`host_waiter_sleep`) and the pump
 signals only the satisfied ones (`host_waiters_wake`, Sep 26); a
 `cond_broadcast` to 150 handlers per cycle was ~20 % of efsd in
 futex/spinlock. Any new wait-for-view goes through a slot, not a
 shared condvar.
- **One KV WAL fsync per pump cycle** (`sync_hold` around drain+tick,
 release before `persist_applied`, `84a2a55`): per-put fsync was 0.5 ms
 per applied entry under `h->mu` on every replica. Spec: the Raft log is
 the durability boundary; the KV must be durable only before the saved
 applied index advances. No per-apply fsync; no snapshot past the KV's
 durable point.
- **The compactor's L0 back-pressure wait is on the pump (Sep 29
  04:27Z trace).** `kv_maybe_flush_locked` waits on `l->cv` while
  `n_l0 + 16 > 64`; the caller is the apply path under `h->mu`, so a
  1.8 GB range-0 rewrite (24 s) is a 24 s pump hold: no heartbeats,
  `host_wait_applied` 400 ms timeouts (`apply-sleep`), REPORT BUSY,
  client fsync EIO. The compactor's segment `fsync` is the 100 ms mode
  (its own 200–300 MB of dirty pages); the pump's Raft-log `fsync` is
  0.4 ms. A snapshot export is no longer in the picture (W22.1).
  Compaction shape is a design decision (D9/D10) — ask.
- **L1 compaction is a background thread (W13, Sep 26).** An L0 still
 spans every shard, so each cycle rewrites all of L1 (~760 MiB, ~2 s
 on the live table). The merge uses private segment opens and holds
 no `l->mu` and no `h->mu`; the pump only swaps the manifest. A
 segment file is unlinked when its pin count hits 0, so a pinned
 view (and a lookup pread) keeps the old file. `apply_max` during
 those 2 s rewrites was 68 ms. The write path blocks only if `n_l0`
 hits 64 (`kv-compact: backpressure`). `kv_compact_locked` remains
 the synchronous path for tests and for a thread that failed to
 start. Do not put compaction back under the pump. Partitioned
 flush is the unread follow-on. A point get still pins the segment
 and preads outside `l->mu` (`5d3e603`).
- **An AppendEntries success reply reports `prev_index + nentries`,
  never the follower's last log index (Sep 30, `raft.c on_ae_req`).**
  A follower's uncommitted stale tail at the leader's next index
  answered an empty heartbeat with `match = last_i`; the leader took
  it, committed its own term-41 no-op (437638) with a quorum that did
  not hold it, and every follower-served group-0 read waited for that
  index and returned BUSY (rsync stat 16.76 s → ENOENT, `ls` EBUSY).
  Second half of the same wedge: `append_local` does not move
  `send_idx`; the leader no-op, cold entry, and joint entry go
  through `send_idx_cover` now, or `send_ae` never ships them
  (`results/measure/20260930-032400-g0-wedge`, `test_raft`
  `test_noop_over_stale_tail`). A follower waiting in `apply-sleep`
  for an index above its own `last_i` on a stable term is this class:
  gdb the leader's `send_idx`/`match_index` before anything else.
- **A Raft proposal is identified by `(index, term)`, never by index
  (I17 root cause, fixed `46d54e6`):** the apply ring matched by index;
  after a leader change the truncated entry's index is reused and the
  old leader's coordinator read the new entry's OK as "DECIDE COMMIT
  applied" → RESOLVE COMMIT on one shard, recovery ABORT on the rest =
  ino 62991 (nlink=3 nents=1, 0 dentries), ino 31264 (dentry → missing
  row), and the forced repro `tests/measure/i17_leader_freeze.sh` on
  `84a2a55` (`results/measure/20260922-042153-i17-leader-freeze`).
 Now: term mismatch → NOT_PRIMARY, ring slot overwritten → BUSY, never
 OK; `raft-obs` prints `arc_term_miss=`. Any new wait-for-apply must go
 through `host_wait_verdict` / `host_wait_settled`, not a bare
 `host_wait_applied` + ring read. Pre-fix leftovers on 19810 stay
 half-applied (no repair tool); dump with `tests/tools/kv_dir_dump` on
 a KV COPY (fcstor004/005 hold every shard) before blaming new code.
- **A retried dir op is answered from its op-id window (I16, `43bdf6a`):**
 the window's shard is the dentry shard of the destination name, never
 the coordinator (randomized) or the parent shard (may be the other
 group). Record the verdict in the same txn (`host_prep_opid`,
 `EFS_TXN_REDUCE_OPID`) or the same KV batch (`opid_item`) as the op —
 never as a separate write, and never make the apply return the
 pre-check error (EEXIST/ENOENT) itself; the leader's pre-check runs
 after `host_opid_replay`. The client holds one in-flight slot across
 the whole `rpc_send_recv_*` retry loop so every retry is byte-identical;
 a new RPC = a new seq. Do not add a client-side "stop retrying BUSY".
- **A hintless NOT_PRIMARY (`primary_id == 0`) is an election in
 progress, not an error** (Sep 23): a stale leader that just stepped down
 answers NOT_PRIMARY with no hint for up to a heartbeat or two; the client
 used to return `EFS_ERR_NOT_PRIMARY` at once, `efs_client_stat_ino` maps
 every RPC failure to NOT_FOUND, and the FUSE `*_at` helpers map that to
 ENOENT — so `mkdir` on a dir that had just committed returned ENOENT
 (`ll_mkdir` → `lookup_fill(new_ino)` GETATTR; freeze run 2,
 `results/measure/20260923-025903-i17-leader-freeze`). Now
  `rpc_send_recv_shard/_dual` back off like BUSY. **Sep 29: the steady
  source of hintless NOT_PRIMARY on a stable cluster is
  `host_read_inode_lanes` on a single-group host** (a foreign lane's
  `host_read_index` returns `!v.has` → hint −1, after the op committed:
  setattr, rename, utimens, unlink, link, getattr callers). It cost
  50 ms per hit on the skip-the-voter client and 16× → 10 s EBUSY
  before (the 810 `exhausted` lines in fstor007's ecopy log). Fix is
  START-HERE §1b 20:55Z item 2 (hint a dual host, check before
  propose). An ENOENT/EIO from a
 FUSE dir op whose target exists is a reply-path RPC failure first —
 grep `fuse: mkdir ... but getattr` / `inode-rpc:` in `fuse.log` before
 suspecting metadata.
- **A COMMITted directory entry is visible before RESOLVE** (Sep 23,
 `lookup_tx`): handler lookup and resolve read through an EXCL intent
 whose coordinator has COMMITted (`efs_meta_apply_lookup_tx` /
 `resolve_tx`), same rule as `get_inode_tx` for the inode row. The
 apply path stays on the plain read. A negative lookup pays one extra
 local get of the intent key; the coordinator is asked only when an
 intent exists. `rmdir`/`unlink` map `EFS_ERR_BUSY` to EBUSY. The 16-
 attempt client budget (~10 s) is shorter than a freeze's recovery
 (16 s on shard 1504); do not widen it.
- **A queued posix test cannot time out** (`posix_suite.py` stamped the
 clock at submit until `a683def`): every 9-host number before Sep 21
 22:00 was that artefact plus real stalls. Compare only against later runs.
- Rolling restarts trigger ~5 min of group elections afterwards: peer
 conns are RDMA (`peer_pool.c`), a restarted node's QPs die on its peers
 with `transport retry counter exceeded` and block senders. Pre-existing,
 converges; not the flow-control bug (that one is fixed).
- **An RDMA peer lane waits 30 s for a RAFT_REPLY; TCP waits 250 ms
 (Sep 30, `results/measure/20260930-040600-postfix-review`).**
 `host_sender` sends a batch and blocks per reply; the
 `HOST_SEND_IO_MS` bound is set only `if (pc->kind == EFS_CONN_TCP)`,
 so an RDMA conn goes through `conn_rdma_frame` →
 `efs_rdma_recv_wait(EFS_IO_TIMEOUT_MS)`. Signature on `raft-obs
 tx->N`: `sent` frozen across 5 s samples while `enq` grows, then
 `fail` +1; the peer keeps processing the batch (it grants votes
 through a frozen lane) but its reply is late. Every unheard peer
 campaigns and `on_vote_req` → `maybe_step_down` deposes the live
 leader (no Pre-Vote, no stickiness): 426 group-2 terms in 13 min
 under a 400-thread ecopy, each cycle 400 ms of BUSY for every
 client read (`wait_timeouts` 0→1290 on fcstor004, 21 LOOKUPs and 8
 REPORTs exhausted, `newfstatat` up to 59.5 s). A term storm with
 `apply_max` under 100 µs and no CQE-error line is this class: read
 the `tx->` lines before the pump. **F1 and F2 are in tree (05:06Z
 roll):** `efs_conn.recv_timeout_ms` / `efs_conn_set_recv_timeout()`
 bound `conn_rdma_frame`'s wait, `host_sender` sets it to
 `HOST_SEND_IO_MS` on every conn kind; `server_raft_host_inbox`
 counts drops (`raft-host: inbox full` for the first ten,
 `inbox_drop=` on the obs line). A stalled lane now shows as `fail`
 +1 every 250 ms, not a 30 s freeze. **The reply was never lost
 (Sep 30 06:17Z, `results/measure/20260930-060000-dd-wedge`):** a
 frame over 72 KiB goes over the conn's TCP side-channel and is
 answered on TCP, smaller ones on RDMA; `host_sender` pipelines up to
 three messages and read the replies with `recv_chan` left at the
 last message's channel, so [AE_REP (RDMA), big AE (TCP)] read TCP
 only and left the RDMA reply in the ring → timeout → conn destroyed
 → same window resent → same shape. Only the fcstor004↔fcstor005
 lane mixes one group's AEs with the other's replies, and only a
 big-entry stream (publish batches of a 100 GiB dd) makes the AE
 take TCP; both directions failed every 250 ms for 10 min
 (fail=2399, hi=2048), the fed follower sat ~700 entries behind, and
 every follower-served read / dual-group REPORT was BUSY. Fixed
 `85f5b31c`: `efs_conn.last_recv_chan`, replies counted per channel.
 A `tx->N fail` that grows by exactly 4/s with `hi=2048` and stable
 terms is a sender-side read on the wrong channel, not the wire.
 Stickiness (D15) and a separate peer credit class (D16) are asks
 without their motivating case.
- Raft catch-up: follower dedupes same index+term entries; leader keeps
  one AE batch in flight per peer (`ae_inflight`). Before this, 005
  rewrote the same 128-entry window ~30×/s with an fsync each and never
  caught up. A restarted leader re-exports the snapshot blob on demand;
  `disk_save_snap` accepts skip-ahead (InstallSnapshot onto a behind
  follower). Salt is replicated via `EFS_MD_CMD_SALT`.
- **Snapshot install must be a sorted diff.** `efs_kv_group_import`
  used to scan every local key against the whole image and then PUT
  every key, on the pump under `h->mu`. On the first chunked install
  that held fcstor003 and fcstor004 for hours (`incoming_has` in
  `kv_snap.c`). A live group that already matches the image writes
  nothing. Do not put that scan back. The export itself stays on the
  GC thread; `apply_max` during a 386 MB export was 0. While that
 export is in progress `send_snap` returns BUSY; `send_ae` must still
 emit an empty AppendEntries or the peer campaigns and steps the
 leader down (Sep 27 group 2, term +200, 9-host mkdir EBUSY).
- `host_pump` ticks are wall-clocked (`HOST_TICK_US`), never per iteration
  (iteration-counted ticks caused election storms). The network inbox path
  must never take `h->mu` (eventfd kick, not condvar). Forwarded-command
  replies read the apply-result ring at the command's OWN index
  (`host_propose_wait_idx`), never `efs_raft_applied()`.
- Server partial-commit on STALE in REPORT (skip stale recs, commit the
  rest, reply STALE) + client bulk repull/replay of only moved chunks
  (`stale_repull_replay`) is what broke the shared-file fsync livelock.
  Raft was never the bottleneck there (commit==applied throughout).
- `EFS_MSG_INODE_LOOKUP` reply carries the full row; LOOKUP_PATH batches
 ≤64 ancestors; readdir cursor is `(src, name)`, not `after_ino`.
- **`group_slot` returns a slot for every attached group; "not hosted"
 is `!g->hosted`, never `!g` (Oct 1).** The forwarded-command path
 (`host_propose` → `host_remote_cmd` → `host_wait_verdict`) read the
 unhosted group's empty apply ring on a single-group host and answered
 BUSY with `arc_miss` +1 per call — `raft-mkfs` rc=-13 on every node
 of a fresh cluster (the SALT step on group 2 from fcstor003). The
 leader's submit reply is the verdict for a forwarded command.
- **`raft-mkfs` once, on one node (Oct 1, START-HERE 0d / W37).** Each
 node proposes its own `h->salt`; retrying the mkfs on another node
 while the SALT step is BUSY gives group 0 one salt and group 2's
 anchor another → `efs_meta_apply_salt_record` PROTO → `apply salt
 rc=-7 index=3` on every group-2 apply forever; only a wipe recovers.
 `roll_efsd.sh --all --fresh` waits for both leaders and mkfs's on
 fcstor003 only. The server-side guard is open.
- **The client's table is not an emptiness witness (Oct 1).**
  `efs_client_unlink` refused `rmdir` with ENOTEMPTY when
  `efs_export_dir_empty` found a child in the local table — a child
  whose removal committed elsewhere (another client, a retry answered
  from the op-id window) stays listed, and one host of nine failed
  `dir_deep_nesting` for minutes while the server's row was empty
  (fcstor012 Sep 30 `d50`, fcstor009 Oct 1 `d25`). The check now logs
  `efs: rmdir … local table lists child` and the server decides
  (`EFS_INODE_RPC_NOT_EMPTY`). A one-host ENOTEMPTY with a clean KV is
  this line in that client's fuse.log, not metadata.
- **`raft-mkfs` replicates the COMMITTED salt, never the caller's
  (W37, in tree, unrolled).** `efs_meta_apply_mkfs` is a no-op on an
  existing root (first salt kept — keep that contract; an EXIST-
  returning variant broke `test_meta_apply`/`test_sim`); the fork was
  the SALT step carrying the second node's `h->salt`. `server_raft_
  host_mkfs` now reads the salt back (`efs_meta_apply_export_salt`),
  BUSY if this node holds no record; SALT is ring-only (PROTO is a
  verdict, not a halted group).
- **`apply_unlink_cmd` / `apply_rmdir_cmd` map NOT_FOUND at apply to
  OK with no log line.** A rename of the same source racing a
 log-path unlink can make both return 0 and leave the dest dentry
 dangling (`-?????????`, posix2 `peer_rename_vs_unlink_src`, 1 in
 6, START-HERE 0c / W36). Do not add more silent "replay" mappings;
 a committed-replay verdict must come from the op-id window.

- **A span record's identity is (object gen, off, len), never the gen
 alone (Sep 30, `results/io500/20260930-183504-rdma`).** The object
 name is a content hash of the 128 KiB PUT image; two clients that
 merge a shared chunk to the same image PUT one object under two
 ranges. `apply_publish` took the second range as "replay of this
 same span object" and the REPORT pack skipped it as held
 (`efs_meta_apply_chunk_holds` now takes off/len; a full image is held
 only by the base gen). Every ior-hard-read loss on Sep 30 (40–208
 records per run, always the client-boundary piece of a chunk shared
 by two hosts, server row = the peer's range under the missing
 client's gen, no apply line, `report-split skip=N`) was this. A rec
 the pack "skips" must be provably in the row at the rec's range.
- **The client table conflates "server row" with "our unreported
 PUT"** (`dcache_put_now` sets the table gen to the candidate and adds
 our span to the list). Three consumers were fooled: the STALE
 classifier (`ours == ce.generation → done`), `span_of` (our own span
 "covered" a range, so the next flush published only the tail and
 putid — one object per chunk — forgot the first span), and the
 replay's fetch (read our own object back as the committed base).
 Now `pull_chunks_range` reports chunks the host has NO ROW for
 (absence bitmap, `efs_client_pull_chunks_range_absent`); such a chunk
 is never "committed", its replay starts from zeros with an empty
 observation and expected gen 0 (`dcache_replay_stale_ex`), and
 `span_of` ignores the putid object's own span. Do not neutralize the
 table entry outside that replay: reads between PUT and REPORT use it.

**Client / FUSE**
- **A dcache store installs have_base/base_gen/range under the same
 lock as the buffer (`dcache_init`, Sep 30).** `dcache_take` marks the
 slot dirty; the second locked section of `dcache_load_and_patch` /
 `dcache_load_sparse` came after, and a flusher in between snapshotted
 `dirty=1 nrange=0` (`snap-inner | dirty=1 hb=1 bg=0 obj=0 r=`), PUT
 the buffer as a full image, and the merge overlay (walks nrange)
 dropped the write. Any new store path goes through `dcache_init`.
- Timeouts stay **0** (entry/attr/negative). `notify_inval_*` from a
  request handler deadlocks — queue after reply on a dedicated thread.
  `FOPEN_DIRECT_IO` on every open; libfuse 3.10.2 cannot emit
  `PARALLEL_DIRECT_WRITES` or `FUSE_MAX_PAGES`.
- `dcache_image_current`: a cached whole image is served only when the map
  names the same generation (`tg != 0 && tg == base_gen`) or the slot is
  explicitly dirty/unpublished. `snap_seq`/`object_seq` order concurrent
  PUTs of one chunk; `dcache_install_image` overlays in-flight dirty
  ranges. Truncate drops rdcache + clean dcache for the dropped chunks.
- A read whose layout pull fails must FAIL, not zero-fill (I9). The old
  1/s rate-limited `pull_layout_miss` returned 38054 zero 1 MiB reads in
  1.36 s and looked like 4 GiB/s.
- **A utimens between the last `write()` and `close()` is lost: mtime
 becomes the close time (Sep 30,
 `results/measure/20260930-044100-mtime-utimens-close`).** The write
 is client-buffered, its PUBLISH arrives in the close REPORT after the
 SETATTR, carries `p.now` and the current `mtime_gen`, and the lane
 stamp wins the stat MAX (`meta_apply.c:3467`, `:4175`). ecopy's
 `write→futimens→close→rename` hits it on every file (153 "metadata
 mismatch (mtime)" lines, and every already-copied file is re-copied
 on the next run); rsync's `write→close→utimens` does not.
 `EFS_INO_REC_F_TIMES` is set by the client and read by no server code;
 `MTIME_PIN_MAX` 256 drops pins silently. Repro: `mtime_repro.py` in
 that dir, one process, idle cluster. **Fixed 05:06Z (client
 side):** `efs_fuse_utimens_ino` → `efs_utimens_flush_dirty` runs the
 close path's `efs_append_flush_report` before a SETATTR that sets
 mtime when the inode has writeback pending or is in the dirty set;
 the publish lands under the old `mtime_gen` and the utimens fences
 it. Gate: `mtime_repro.py` rows `ecopy-order*` and `fsync-between`
 print 1380661863 (`~/efs-runs/rec-gate84.log`). The server still
 reads nothing from `EFS_INO_REC_F_TIMES`; `efs_client_mtime_pin`
 now logs once when its table is full. Not F1/F2, not the election
 storm.
- **"Nothing to flush" has three sources, and the dirty SET is not one
 of them (Sep 30, `results/measure/20260930-080000-ecopy-times-review`).**
 Unpublished bytes live in (a) the wb queue + `busy_ino[]` (a job
 waiting in `wb_overlap_inflight` must already be in `busy_ino`), (b) a
 dirty dcache entry (`efs_dcache_ino_pinned`), (c) a PUT window — every
 flusher marks the entry clean before the PUT and the chunk dirty after
 it (`put_win_open/close`, `efs_dcache_flush_ino` waits). The dirty set
 is what a REPORT snapshot consumes; a threshold REPORT ships an
 irec-only record and leaves the bytes in (b). Any "flush before X"
 (utimens, truncate, a future fsync shortcut) checks all three or X
 overtakes the publish (mtime = close time). A chunk rec without an
 irec makes the server size the file to the chunk end and it never
 shrinks: mark the ino in the same locked block as the chunk. The
 staged row has `atime_nsec`/`ctime_nsec` now; copy them wherever a
 row is copied. `EFS_DCACHE_TRACE=1` prints `utimens`/`report`/
 `release` lines beside the slot transitions — read those before
 theorizing about a time mismatch.
- **A client RPC at exactly 5.0 / 10 / 15 s with `attempts=1
 saw_busy=0` is the conn pool, not the network (Sep 30,
 `results/measure/20260930-051000-review2`).** `efs_client_conn_get`
 waits 5 s on a full per-node pool and returns NULL; a checkout holds
 a slot for the whole RPC, a PUT holds three. Until `e002771e` the
 pool was 16 per node, the timeout was silent, and `put_fragments` /
 `fetch_fragment` counted the NULL as a node failure — four made a
 live node DOWN for 30 s (`EFS_NODE_DOWN_FAILS`/`EFS_NODE_DOWN_MS`),
 which was the 28 s client-wide freezes under a 400-thread ecopy and
 the `no quorum` PUTs against four live servers. Now 64 per node
 (`EFS_CLIENT_CONNS_PER_NODE` overrides; server cap 4096), logged as
 `efs: conn pool node=N exhausted for 5 s`, never counted. A NULL from
 `conn_get` is never a reason to call `efs_client_node_note_fail`
 (connect failures are counted inside). `dcache_reclaim_main` at the
 top of a client profile under fresh-file writes is the same review:
 the threads now park when a sweep pops nothing.
- **A stall on every client RPC exactly once a second is the staging
 evictor, not the servers (Sep 30,
 `results/measure/20260930-063500-perf-dir-review`).**
 `stage_evict_main` wakes on a 1 s timedwait; once the staging
 estimate is over `EFS_CLIENT_META_MB` (256 MB) it evicts 64 rows
 per pass under the client table lock. Each `evict_one` ran three
 walks over every loaded shard tab (2400 of 4096 after one du,
 250 µs) and `efs_export_compact` rebuilt every tab's 512-slot index
 to the same size: 14–27 ms per second with every RPC thread queued
 on the lock (strace: 58 futex hand-offs on `g_client` after the
 `ETIMEDOUT <1.000>`). Fixed: `efs_export_evict_ino` visits the tabs
 the row names, compact reindexes only when the rebuild shrinks,
 slab bytes are booked at `sizeof(struct efs_inode_mem)`
 (`test_stage_evict`). Two du runs after: 0 syscalls over 10 ms.
  Still true (D18, ask): the per-tab floor (~90–170 KB × up to 4096
  tabs) is above the cap before any data is staged, so the evictor
  churns 64 hot rows a second and never reaches it (412 MB estimate,
  330 MB RSS at 10780 rows). Trace the client (`strace -f -T` on
  `efs-fuse`, look for the 1 s `ETIMEDOUT`) before the servers for
  any 1 Hz pattern. **Under load it is not 1 Hz (Sep 30 21:10Z,
  `results/measure/20260930-205300-ecopy-perf-review`):** every
  `ensure_meta_room` kicks the thread and a pass that evicted 64 rows
  re-arms at once, so during an ecopy the inlined `evict_pass` LRU
  scan is 47.6 % of client cycles (31.6 % of a 62 min profile), each
  pass taking the table lock 64× plus a compact. **D18 decided Oct 1
  (user): evict whole cold tabs.** After the four row bands, if the
  table is still over the cap, `evict_cold_tabs` (stage_evict.c) takes
  the 256 least recently used tabs (`efs_export_tabs_by_age` on
  `shard_tick`), and under table + idx + dirty locks drops up to 64 of
  them whose every row/chunk ino passes evict_one's pin rules
  (`efs_export_tab_for_each_ino` + `tab_pin_cb`; LRU entries of the
  dropped inos removed under `g_lru_mu`; `efs_export_drop_tab` frees
  the tab and its `staged_est`). Staging a row rebuilds the tab empty
  (`efs_export_table`); readers and in-place updaters do not —
  `chunk_tab_peek` (get_chunk, set_chunk_gen, set_chunk_deltas,
  add_chunk_delta) and `shard_route` (set_size/mode/owner/mtime/atime)
  return NOT_FOUND on a dropped tab instead of allocating ~100 KB for
  a miss. `test_stage_evict` covers it. `TAB_SCAN`/`TAB_EVICT` are
  internal constants. The row LRU is unchanged for the rest.
- **`close(2)` of any file walks `size / 128 KiB` dcache slots
  (Sep 30 21:10Z, same review).** `ll_flush → efs_append_flush_report
  → efs_dcache_flush_ino → dcache_flush_ino_pass` calls
  `dcache_steal_dirty` per chunk index under the append stripe, on
  read-only opens and dup'd fds too, and walks all 65536 slots once
  the file is ≥ 8 GiB. It was 36.9 % of the client's cycles during an
  `ecopy --verify`. **Fixed `65237a59` (Oct 1, clients fcstor007–015
  + fstor007 `/tmp/efs`):** `efs_append_flush_report` returns at once
  when `efs_ino_has_unpublished` (wb pending, dirty set, dcache pin —
  the utimens predicate, shared) is 0. **`b6c1712d` (Oct 1):** the
  dcache row pin is released when a PUT lands and the entry is clean
  (`dcache_flush_keep`, `dcache_flush_slot_inner`; the dirty set holds
  the row until the REPORT) — before, `dcache_pin_release` ran only on
  drop/reclaim, so every file this client wrote stayed pinned
  (unevictable, "has unpublished") while its slot lived. Same commit:
  `dcache_flush_ino_pass` flushes the inode's entries found on the
  per-shard dirty lists (`dcache_dirty_cis_of`) instead of walking
  `ci = 0..size/128 KiB`; the reclaim pop sets `reclaim_claimed`
  instead of unlinking a still-dirty entry, so dirty ⟹ on_dirty holds
  and the lists are a complete index (an unlinked-but-dirty entry was
  invisible to a close racing the pop). `dcache_flush_all_slots` is
  reclaim-all only. Prior commit: `dcache_store`/`dcache_store_owned` reuse a
  dead chained node (`dcache_chain_reuse`) instead of growing the
  chain — the reclaim flush zeroes ino/body of a chained node and
  nothing reused or unlinked it. Chain length was never counted
  (`efs_dcache_yield_extra` = one find = 6.6 % was the evidence);
  the gdb walk in the review SUMMARY still applies if it stays hot.
- **`st_blocks` is 0 for every regular file this client did not
 write** (`du` 52K for 3.8 TB = 103 symlinks × 512 B; ecrawl
 `files_sparse_heuristic` = nearly all). `inode_allocated_bytes`
 counts the client-local present-chunk table (W20), empty since D2
 for unopened files; the row has no chunk count and W20 forbids
 size-based `st_blocks`. Design ask D17 — do not fake it from size.
- `adopt_rpc_inode` drops dcache only on SHRINK; dropping on
  same-size/newer-mtime discarded the only replay source of an in-flight
  O_APPEND object (`concurrent_appends` lost lines).
- `.fuse_hidden*` is libfuse's silly-rename (unlink/rename of an open
  file); readdir hides the prefix; `hard_remove` was rejected.
- Client on-CPU under writes is blake3 in `hash_write_fragments`; the wall
  is off-CPU (fsync/REPORT). Optimizing blake3 does not move the number.

**RDMA**
- Auto/RDMA first-`mkdir` hang on an EMPTY table: root cause was the client
  closing a pooled conn's TCP side-channel (fd recycled) so the server
  reaped the QP. Pool identity fix is in tree (`test_conn_fd`), live gate
  needs an EMPTY table — `tests/rdma_first_inode.sh` is one (private
  3-node cluster on fcstor007, ports 19950–19952; W10 recommends this,
  not a wipe of 19810). RNR-NAK / recv-buffer hypothesis is
  **dead**. A populated table drove millions of creates over RDMA
  with 0 errors. The Sep 27 live switch of 19810 failed the 9-host
  posix (many-op tests hit 15 s; `apply_max` under 1 ms) and was
  rolled back. Cause, fixed in tree and not rolled: the shared recv
  poller slept 100 ms after acking a CQ event, and every SEND called
  `ibv_query_qp` plus a sysfs read (~380 µs raft RTT vs ~15 µs TCP).
  Private 100-mkdir afterward was 642 ms vs 507 ms TCP. Default
  stays TCP. Do not debug that on the live cluster.

- **`getifaddrs` per RDMA conn upgrade was 26 % of efs-fuse cycles
 (Oct 1 20 GiB dd, `osq_lock` 20 %):** `dev_for_fd` resolved local
 IP → ifname → HCA through `getifaddrs` + sysfs on every new conn,
 and a client's first PUT window opens 256 conns at once, all
 spinning on rtnl. Cached per local IPv4 under `g_dev_lock`, one
 resolver at a time. If that symbol is back in a profile, conns are
 being re-created — count `efs_client_conn_drop` first.
- **A condvar that every completion signals under its mutex is a herd
  (Oct 1, `g_reclaim`):** `dcache_kick_complete` locked `g_reclaim.mu` +
  `cond_signal` per completed chunk, every sweep end `cond_broadcast` to
  16 workers — 156 K futex calls per 3 s on two addresses, 18 % of all
  client cycles in `native_queued_spin_lock_slowpath`, the FUSE thread
  queued in `ll_write_buf → __lll_lock_wait`. Now `kicks` is an atomic
  counter and `reclaim_kick` takes the mutex only when `waiters > 0`.
  Client CPU for a 20 GiB dd 74.9 → 33.6 s; the wall did not move
  (dd's serial source read + the close REPORT). Resolve futex
  addresses with `nm -n /tmp/efs/efs-fuse` (non-PIE), not gdb through
  ssh. Any new pool wakeup follows `reclaim_kick`.
- **`perf record -p <efs-fuse>` taken at mount sees only the 7 initial
  threads**; the reclaim/put pools are created at the first write and
  never show (no blake3 in the user's `client.sh --perf` profile).
  `perf record -a` is REFUSED at `perf_event_paranoid=1` (Oct 1; the
  fio-honest rule's "-a works" is wrong) — attach `-p` ~0.7 s after the
  I/O starts so the pools exist; `--sort pid` / `--tid` for one thread
  (this perf has no `--sort tid`); run `perf report` on the node
  (the login-node perf cannot read the file).
- **FUSE requests are 1 MiB** (`read(/dev/fuse)=1048656`, reply
  `writev=1048592`): libfuse 3.10.2 does negotiate `FUSE_MAX_PAGES`
  from `max_write`. A 1 MiB `write()` is one `ll_write_buf` of eight
  chunks; `ll_read` answers 1 MiB from a thread-local buffer (was a
  `malloc`/`mmap` + kernel zeroing per READ), or — chunk-aligned and
  every chunk cached and clean — with `fuse_reply_iov` over pinned
  rdcache images (`efs_client_read_refs`, Oct 1). `fuse_reply_data`
  with a multi-buffer bufvec COPIES into a fresh allocation in 3.10;
  `fuse_reply_iov` does not. A pinned way (`rdcache_ent.pins`) is never
  a victim and `rdcache_put` leaves its bytes; unpin after the reply.
- **The read path's cost was copies, and the in-flight depth is the
  wall (Oct 1, `agent-rd-20261001-*`).** Cold 16 GiB single stream
  2.5 → 3.6 GB/s (CPU 21.6 → 10.8 s) after: prefetched chunk handed to
  the rdcache (`efs_rdcache_put_owned`), whole-chunk demand decoded into
  the caller's buffer (`chunk_get_job.ext`), data fragments received
  into the chunk (`efs_decode_chunk` skips the self-copy), iov reply.
  Four readers 3.7 → 6.5 GB/s with 64 get workers (`GET_POOL_N`; one
  synchronous fetch per worker = the client's in-flight cap, was 32).
  `EFS_READ_PREFETCH=32` is slower than 16. Left: RDMA recv buffer →
  chunk (zero-copy receive is a transport change — ask) and W40. Do not
  chase blake3 or `xor_into` on the read side.
- **W39: an RDMA send of a bufpool buffer is two SGEs, no copy**
  (`efs_rdma_zc_region_add` per slab, lazy `ibv_reg_mr` per HCA,
  `max_send_sge = 2`, payload ≥ 4 KiB). The payload must live until the
  send CQE — true today because a conn ends replied or destroyed. Any
  new sender that frees or reuses the buffer before the reply breaks
  this; `efs_rdma_send_quiesce` is the reap for a destroy path.
  `cat … | dd skip=N` on a pipe without `iflag=fullblock` skips short
  pipe reads — compare against a regular file (gate670's CMP_TAIL_BAD).
- **efs-fuse pins itself to the HCA's NUMA node** (`numa_pin_startup`,
  `efs: numa pin node=N cpus=M (hca …)` in fuse.log; `EFS_NUMA_NODE=
  none|N`). fstor007: HCA on node 2 of 8, cold remote-node memcpy
  3–18 GB/s vs 55–66 hot; 20 GiB dd write 20.1 → 18.9 s, read 12.2 →
  6.6 s (`~/orcd/scratch/efs/perf/agent-dd-20261001-*-numa`). fcstor:
  2 nodes, HCAs on node 0 → CPUs 0–11. Pin means affinity + MPOL_PREFERRED,
  set before the first thread; a pin after threads exist binds one
  thread only.
- **A client whose dd's sit in D-state in `request_wait_answer` while
  efs-fuse is idle (all threads asleep, FUSE workers in `read`,
  `/sys/fs/fuse/connections/N/waiting=0`) is waiting on ONE RPC's retry
  loop, not on the kernel or the servers (Oct 1 13:41Z, fstor007, 8 ×
  20 GiB dd + ecopy).** The close-time REPORT of a 20 GiB file was
  163 840 records; eight at once exceeded the 30 s recv timeout and
  `rpc_send_recv_dual` re-sent the whole REPORT (`retry type=67
  why=recv rc=-6`, `slow-ok attempts=6 us=116 s`); every other close
  on the client queued on `report_mu` (ecopy "never started"); SIGKILL
  cannot interrupt the kernel's forced FLUSH. **D24 (decided by the
  user Oct 1):** `report_landed_note` REPORTs the dirty set every 8192
  landed PUTs (`report-landed:` line), so close publishes ≤ 1 GiB; the
  REPORT reply wait is `30 s + 0.5 ms × records`. Gate: 4 × 8 GiB dd
  on one client 9.8 s (3.5 GB/s) with a concurrent 300-file
  create/close storm at p99 51 ms (`agent-wedge-20261001-141222`).
  `report_mu` is still one lock per client (W41, ask). Diagnose this
  class with `gdb -p` + `ps -o wchan` + the log, no strace needed.
- **Both daemons stamp every log line** (`src/common/log_ts.c`,
  `2026-10-01T14:01:46.414Z ` prefix via `fopencookie` on fds 1/2,
  `EFS_LOG_TS=0` off). `fileno(stderr)` is -1 in the daemons — write
  raw bytes to fd 2. All harness greps are substring matches.
- **Remounting through `scripts/client.sh` right after `stop` fails with
  "another efs-fuse already holds"** — the flock fd is inherited by the
  dying daemon; poll `pgrep -x efs-fuse` empty before the next mount.
- **Clean dcache bodies have no budget and no evictor** (freed only by
 drop or take-replace). Do not keep a full-overwrite body after its
 PUT "as cache": RSS then tracks the bytes written (D23, ask).
- **A chunk inside a PUT window is readable nowhere (Oct 1).** The
 full-image snap steals the body (`snap-steal-full`) and the local
 map names the object only after the PUT lands; W30 PUTs completed
 chunks during the write, so a read in between zero-filled a "hole"
 (`efs: read hole`, posix `writev_readv_chunk_straddle` got `\0\0\0`
 for `XYZ`). `efs_client_read` waits on `efs_dcache_put_win_wait`
 first (lock-free when `put_win_total` is 0). `read hole` lines also
 come from legitimate sparse reads — only a failing non-sparse test
 makes them evidence. Any new reader path (prefetch, verify) goes
 through the same wait.
- **rdcache `pending` marks live on data-less ways; `rdcache_find`
 skips those (Oct 1).** `efs_rdcache_put` used `rdcache_find`, stored
 in another way, and the orphan mark made the next reader of that
 chunk wait forever (posix `basic_pread_pwrite` D-state in
 `request_wait_answer`, 190 NOTRUN, first reread after a fresh
 mount). Every lookup that must see a pending entry matches
 `ino/ci && (data || pending)`; a put or cancel always clears the
 mark, including on alloc failure.

**Harness / measurement**
- IO-500 harness detaches `prterun` (ssh timeout killed the driver);
  `ior-easy|hard-write|verify` run IOR directly for cold verifies (the
  io500 driver deletes its data). Kill hung ranks with `pkill -9 -x io500`
  then remount; never gdb-attach a rank through a timeout'd ssh.
- A 4-client fio storm can drop raft heartbeats (`rc=-15`); recovery is a
  keep-storage efsd bounce, not a wipe. Two 4-client 9×2g jobs exceed the
  400 s ssh budget — do not raise it.
- `dd if=/dev/zero` skips PUTs (all-zero chunks) — use a non-zero source.
- **A remount truncates `/tmp/efs/fuse.log`** (`rm -f` before mount).
 Copy the nine client logs off BEFORE a cold remount for a verify, or
 the run's client evidence is gone (Oct 1 W38). Cold verify of a
 driver run's hard file: remount, then `IOR_HARD_FILE=… IOR_HARD_G=<-G>
 run.sh ior-hard-verify <pairs>` (36 ranks) and
 `tests/tools/hardscan.c` (12 threads, 35 GB in 24 s, prints every bad
 record with offset/chunk/word); `efs-mgmt raft-getchunks <leader>
 <ino> <ci>` with `EFS_MGMT_CHUNKS=1` shows the row and its span list
 (`len=0` = a fold's tombstone).
- posix harness reuses an existing mount (`ensure_mounted`); it remounts
  only on a transport mismatch. 9-way posix hits the 385 s python cap and
  reports `[None]` for never-ran tests (W8) — not 60 bugs.
- Wait for the cluster to be apply-idle (`commit == applied` on all
  voters) before starting a measurement; a run started during catch-up
  measures the catch-up.

